#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115072 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115243 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "853a04b2-591a-51c2-b80f-73f559355d9f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Elementary 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "239ee94c-c3d9-5955-8cdd-afff77ec3279" :question/math-academy-id "e-4716" :question/problem "Is \n$\\begin{aligned}E=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}$ \nan elementary matrix?" :question/worked-solution "An elementary matrix is a matrix obtained from the identity matrix by applying exactly one elementary row operation.\n\nThe given matrix $E$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from $I_2$ by applying the elementary row operation $R_2:= R_2+(-5) R_1,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-5){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =E\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115073 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115244 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6c56b3e9-cf23-5e1e-8c7d-c474059c9a9d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Inverse of an Elementary 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "40cdd39f-29c3-5dad-9708-02c3e0de6acb" :question/math-academy-id "e-4718" :question/problem "Find the inverse of the elementary matrix \n![](images/65/65e27c97ac04ff5514c6e1be3b010e7a6efa464131bfbad03a569f85e728ac85.png)" :question/worked-solution "We write down $E$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply an elementary row operation that transforms the left matrix into the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[E\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{2}{5} & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{2}:=\\frac{5}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & \\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse is obtained on the right-hand side of our big matrix after the transformation. Therefore, \n![](images/cd/cd8839007e548fcd0858ebc42904d3659d639da834b8caa476c4765b5b1a6b69.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115074 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115245 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dd12bc7b-1b5d-5af2-bcef-c9d0fda7d8fc" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Product Involving the Inverse of an Elementary 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "ed71f469-f0e9-5016-846d-cb70d59ab3ea" :question/math-academy-id "e-4719" :question/problem "Find the product $E_1 E_{2}^{-1},$ where ![](images/ad/ad2295d047cfcd60a05c2b155e8c21c511d295e76b0ad4056cf136fe54c272a1.png) and ![](images/29/299ce95f91a5eb4029b2a8512c0b45c24562fac91cc4c70a7e00ecf42c320099.png)" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $E_1$ and $E_2$ are both elementary matrices.\n\nFirst, we need to find $E_2^{-1}.$ We write down $E_2$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply an elementary row operation that transforms the left matrix into the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[{E}_{2}\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n![](images/a8/a8c0d76a5f06d1077eb45b6af4761434164602747f54d9644646b654d319b373.png)\n\nNow, we can find the product $E_1 E_{2}^{-1}\\mathbin{:}$\n![](images/f1/f16a0b0e573cf3a8022f9c62993364a0d708c3a0cff0b4bb4cb0eadde7848f56.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400499 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115243 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "853a04b2-591a-51c2-b80f-73f559355d9f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Elementary 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "69657e5e-ddb2-5905-87d9-718781e63186" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$S$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400501} {:answer/id #uuid "85f6e2e5-5000-5e4e-823a-50db2fc79262" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Q$ and $S$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400502} {:answer/id #uuid "375a7515-6b30-5def-bfb9-8102113b1869" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Q$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400503} {:answer/id #uuid "4d61a059-dddb-5e13-801e-a014bc95f9c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400504} {:answer/id #uuid "d61776a1-23a9-569a-9478-5925bcddef45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ and $Q$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400505}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "69657e5e-ddb2-5905-87d9-718781e63186" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$S$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400501} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce429fdf-6b6f-5359-bf73-1a833c32c68d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400500}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0d49dd3c-2d26-5daf-baa6-13e98e158d10" :question/math-academy-id "q-130772" :question/problem "Which of the following are elementary matrices?\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,Q=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,S=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "An elementary matrix is a matrix obtained from the identity matrix by applying exactly one elementary row operation. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices.\n\n- The matrices $P$ and $Q$ are not elementary matrices because they can't be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying only one elementary row operation.\n\n- The matrix $S$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =S\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, only $S$ is an elementary matrix."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400506 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115243 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "853a04b2-591a-51c2-b80f-73f559355d9f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Elementary 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1494e45b-e9c6-535d-9eba-3790fc3b3525" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400508} {:answer/id #uuid "9c2aeafd-d65d-5e22-bb2e-c7f378129297" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400509} {:answer/id #uuid "15a62809-c816-544c-b95c-2f003c843342" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$  and $B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400510} {:answer/id #uuid "97c97b06-f6db-546d-aa97-0f3a35c8b667" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400511} {:answer/id #uuid "164d8bbe-3777-525f-8440-8bfe402f43f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$  and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400512}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "97c97b06-f6db-546d-aa97-0f3a35c8b667" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400511} :answer-field/id #uuid "70925ade-385f-5362-ac6e-a022a4d19eae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400507}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ea3b1565-0857-5d0a-8afe-805754b2cf2a" :question/math-academy-id "q-130778" :question/problem "Which of the following are elementary matrices?\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}6 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 9\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "An elementary matrix is a matrix obtained from the identity matrix by applying exactly one elementary row operation. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices.\n\n- The matrices $A$ and $C$ are not elementary matrices because they can't be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying only one elementary row operation.\n\n- The matrix $B$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{1}:=4{R}_{1},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{1}:=4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, only $B$ is an elementary matrix."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388160 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115244 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6c56b3e9-cf23-5e1e-8c7d-c474059c9a9d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Inverse of an Elementary 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ef815bd-f246-5489-a173-af59955aa440" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -7 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388162} {:answer/id #uuid "a7b90574-37d3-5939-9d6d-f41a132e44c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{7} & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388163} {:answer/id #uuid "e0797fd8-3780-510c-a616-159309484a73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\frac{1}{7} \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388164} {:answer/id #uuid "dd6a68cc-a941-50cf-87a0-dbba7667c8dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388165} {:answer/id #uuid "2f63056e-6d68-5d2d-a877-8755ac4c2845" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388166}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e0797fd8-3780-510c-a616-159309484a73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\frac{1}{7} \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388164} :answer-field/id #uuid "3e98b29a-38f8-524d-ac20-5c631c095e5d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388161}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6550d7b1-7dc0-5855-b6b5-da0256aaa98e" :question/math-academy-id "q-130798" :question/problem "Find the inverse of the elementary matrix \n$Q=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -\\frac{1}{7} \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "We write down $Q$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply an elementary row operation that transforms the left matrix into the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[Q\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -\\frac{1}{7} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+\\frac{1}{7}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & \\frac{1}{7} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse is obtained on the right-hand side of our big matrix after the transformation. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{Q}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\frac{1}{7} \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265982 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115245 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dd12bc7b-1b5d-5af2-bcef-c9d0fda7d8fc" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Product Involving the Inverse of an Elementary 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0449b553-1b65-5cac-8598-b0d8a270a950" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}21 & -4 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263039} {:answer/id #uuid "08cf208e-4a67-56fb-af8e-a3fd93304b09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}6 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263084} {:answer/id #uuid "79e21f99-e8cc-51c3-a318-a7ddd869b864" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264057} {:answer/id #uuid "7e60b2a6-6056-5ea2-8a17-c6be4b8de34e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}19 & -4 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264091} {:answer/id #uuid "9e4de5ce-9af6-520d-b7aa-ae9f2db45567" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}20 & -7 \\\\ 8 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264350}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0449b553-1b65-5cac-8598-b0d8a270a950" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}21 & -4 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263039} :answer-field/id #uuid "dcf3b9f2-af48-548d-9af3-5530034cb7ad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265636}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d8b759c1-7acc-5a06-82c3-b179ddb571b4" :question/math-academy-id "q-130823" :question/problem "Find the product ${E}_{1}{E}_{2}^{-1}$, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{E}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that ${E}_{1}$ and ${E}_{2}$ are both elementary matrices.\n\nFirst, we need to find ${E}_{2}^{-1}.$ We write down ${E}_{2}$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply an elementary row operation that transforms the left matrix into the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[{E}_{2}\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-5){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -5 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{E}_{2}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we can find the product ${E}_{1}{E}_{2}^{-1}:$\n\n$$\n{E}_{1}{E}_{2}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}21 & -4 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115735 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115245 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dd12bc7b-1b5d-5af2-bcef-c9d0fda7d8fc" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Product Involving the Inverse of an Elementary 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9377ea5e-6172-5098-8da9-28e55afbd440" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-11 & 4 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113377} {:answer/id #uuid "cebc8c59-cf5a-5800-902c-ffd26a334e3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-11 & 4 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 